1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:02,920
In February 2025,

2
00:00:02,770 --> 00:00:07,040
AI researcher and OpenAI co-founder Andrej Karpathy

3
00:00:06,890 --> 00:00:09,980
gave a name to a new way of building software:

4
00:00:09,830 --> 00:00:10,255
vibe coding.

5
00:00:10,705 --> 00:00:13,885
Merriam-Webster now defines it as the practice of using an

6
00:00:13,735 --> 00:00:16,512
artificial intelligence system to generate computer code.

7
00:00:16,962 --> 00:00:18,422
Describe what you want in plain language,

8
00:00:18,272 --> 00:00:21,580
and watch a working app take shape in hours, instead of months.

9
00:00:23,080 --> 00:00:25,423
And vibe coding is no longer a niche experiment.

10
00:00:25,873 --> 00:00:29,034
Around 90% of developers now use AI coding tools.

11
00:00:29,484 --> 00:00:32,527
Roughly 41% of new code is AI-generated.

12
00:00:32,978 --> 00:00:36,318
About 63% of people building with these tools have

13
00:00:36,168 --> 00:00:38,758
no professional background in software engineering.

14
00:00:39,207 --> 00:00:41,547
In Y Combinator's Winter 2025 batch,

15
00:00:41,397 --> 00:00:46,491
a quarter of startups ran on codebases that were roughly 95% AI-generated.

16
00:00:46,941 --> 00:00:50,723
Tens of millions of AI-built apps now exist, and the pace is accelerating.

17
00:00:52,423 --> 00:00:54,963
But building a first version and running a

18
00:00:54,813 --> 00:00:56,940
production system are two very different things.

19
00:00:57,390 --> 00:01:00,030
There's a wide gap between a prototype that demos well,

20
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:01,801
and a workload that can run in production.

21
00:01:02,251 --> 00:01:04,491
We call it the vibe coding cliff:

22
00:01:04,341 --> 00:01:07,251
the moment the excitement of watching your app take shape gives

23
00:01:07,101 --> 00:01:09,898
way to the feeling that the ground has dropped out from under you.

24
00:01:11,498 --> 00:01:14,182
Suddenly, you face questions that never mattered in a demo.

25
00:01:14,632 --> 00:01:17,634
Who owns the infrastructure, and where does the source code live?

26
00:01:18,084 --> 00:01:20,388
How is the app deployed, and how do you roll back?

27
00:01:20,838 --> 00:01:24,180
How do you control networking, identity, encryption, and access?

28
00:01:24,630 --> 00:01:28,198
What happens when traffic spikes, or you hit a platform rate limit?

29
00:01:28,648 --> 00:01:31,419
And what happens if you want to leave the platform you started on?

30
00:01:31,869 --> 00:01:33,569
The stakes are real. By some measures,

31
00:01:33,419 --> 00:01:36,868
close to half of AI-generated code fails basic security testing.

32
00:01:38,768 --> 00:01:41,980
Today, a builder who outgrows a vibe coding platform has two options.

33
00:01:42,430 --> 00:01:46,417
Do it yourself, and spend weeks or months learning cloud, DevOps, and security.

34
00:01:46,867 --> 00:01:50,978
Or hire a developer, and take on the cost of hiring, scoping, and managing the work.

35
00:01:51,428 --> 00:01:52,938
Rethrow is building a third path.

36
00:01:54,638 --> 00:01:56,842
Rethrow is named after a programming concept.

37
00:01:57,292 --> 00:02:00,312
When software hits an error, code can catch it, handle it,

38
00:02:00,162 --> 00:02:03,441
and rethrow it, so it keeps moving forward instead of silently failing.

39
00:02:03,890 --> 00:02:08,010
Your app is the same. When it outgrows the prototype, you don't throw it away.

40
00:02:07,860 --> 00:02:11,320
You catch it, and rethrow it onto dedicated cloud infrastructure.

41
00:02:11,770 --> 00:02:14,554
Our mission is simple: build fast, and own what comes next.

42
00:02:16,754 --> 00:02:18,650
Rethrow bridges the vibe coding cliff.

43
00:02:19,100 --> 00:02:22,026
Move beyond vibe coding, without becoming a cloud engineer.

44
00:02:22,476 --> 00:02:25,076
At launch, there will be two ways to use Rethrow:

45
00:02:24,926 --> 00:02:27,213
migrate an existing app, or build something new.

46
00:02:28,813 --> 00:02:32,493
To migrate, connect an app built on a platform like Lovable,

47
00:02:32,343 --> 00:02:34,717
Replit, or Base44, through GitHub.

48
00:02:35,167 --> 00:02:38,367
Rethrow's migration agents assess your app, understand its architecture,

49
00:02:38,217 --> 00:02:41,303
and design a target environment on your preferred cloud.

50
00:02:41,754 --> 00:02:45,674
Then they write a spec-driven plan, refactor what needs to change,

51
00:02:45,524 --> 00:02:49,353
deploy, and validate the result, while your original app stays untouched.

52
00:02:49,803 --> 00:02:53,443
The goal: turn a highly manual cloud migration into a repeatable,

53
00:02:53,293 --> 00:02:56,239
agent-orchestrated workflow, completed in under an hour.

54
00:02:56,689 --> 00:02:59,602
Migrations will be a one-time price of $500.

55
00:03:01,201 --> 00:03:03,650
Behind the scenes, agents work one spec at a time.

56
00:03:04,100 --> 00:03:06,640
Each spec is a single, logical unit of work,

57
00:03:06,490 --> 00:03:09,017
with its own requirements, design, and tasks.

58
00:03:09,467 --> 00:03:13,827
The agent writes code, deploys, and verifies its work before moving on.

59
00:03:13,677 --> 00:03:18,287
If a check fails, it retries, and escalates to a human when needed.

60
00:03:19,888 --> 00:03:21,726
To build, there's Rethrow Studio.

61
00:03:22,176 --> 00:03:25,876
Keep developing after a migration, or start something completely new,

62
00:03:25,726 --> 00:03:29,026
in a browser interface that feels like the vibe coding you know.

63
00:03:29,475 --> 00:03:32,175
Prompt, preview, iterate, and publish,

64
00:03:32,025 --> 00:03:34,515
while your app runs in a dedicated cloud

65
00:03:34,365 --> 00:03:36,703
environment and source repository you control.

66
00:03:37,154 --> 00:03:40,814
Switch between Vibe and Spec modes, inspect your code and your cloud,

67
00:03:40,664 --> 00:03:43,206
and ship through a fully managed deployment pipeline.

68
00:03:43,656 --> 00:03:46,527
Studio will be $100 per user, per month.

69
00:03:48,227 --> 00:03:51,642
Under the hood, Rethrow Studio runs on a multi-harness control plane.

70
00:03:52,092 --> 00:03:55,692
Most AI development tools sit on top of an agent harness:

71
00:03:55,542 --> 00:03:59,897
the runtime that gives a model access to files, repositories, commands, and tools.

72
00:04:00,347 --> 00:04:03,707
With Rethrow, you bring your own agent: Claude Code, Codex,

73
00:04:03,557 --> 00:04:07,460
Cursor, Gemini CLI, Factory Droid, Goose, Kiro, and more.

74
00:04:07,910 --> 00:04:10,070
Rethrow uses emerging agent protocols,

75
00:04:09,920 --> 00:04:12,870
like the Agent Client Protocol, CLI streaming,

76
00:04:12,720 --> 00:04:16,690
and the Model Context Protocol, to create one uniform experience,

77
00:04:16,540 --> 00:04:18,152
no matter which provider you bring.

78
00:04:18,602 --> 00:04:22,537
Rethrow should remain consistent, even when the underlying agent changes.

79
00:04:22,987 --> 00:04:24,567
It manages every workflow,

80
00:04:24,417 --> 00:04:27,687
from migrations and development to infrastructure operations,

81
00:04:27,537 --> 00:04:30,327
across Amazon Web Services, Google Cloud,

82
00:04:30,177 --> 00:04:32,541
Microsoft Azure, and Oracle Cloud Infrastructure.

83
00:04:34,740 --> 00:04:37,452
We're building Rethrow around six guiding principles.

84
00:04:37,902 --> 00:04:39,842
The first five make an application production-ready.

85
00:04:39,692 --> 00:04:43,193
The sixth keeps it portable, and ready for whatever comes next.

86
00:04:43,643 --> 00:04:47,523
One: Ownership. Everything Rethrow produces belongs to you,

87
00:04:47,373 --> 00:04:50,935
in a dedicated cloud environment and source repository within your control.

88
00:04:51,385 --> 00:04:54,765
Two: Structure. Version-controlled source, infrastructure as code,

89
00:04:54,615 --> 00:04:58,613
repeatable pipelines, and a spec-driven workflow, all managed for you.

90
00:04:59,062 --> 00:05:03,482
Three: Scalability. Cost-efficient, serverless-first architecture by default,

91
00:05:03,332 --> 00:05:06,632
so you pay for real usage, with room to evolve as you grow.

92
00:05:07,083 --> 00:05:11,023
Four: Security. Managed identity, network boundaries, encryption,

93
00:05:10,873 --> 00:05:15,271
least-privilege permissions, and logs you can audit, as a default, not an upgrade.

94
00:05:15,721 --> 00:05:19,621
Five: Reliability. Managed pipelines, automated validation,

95
00:05:19,471 --> 00:05:23,219
and clean rollbacks, so deployments are fast, boring, and repeatable.

96
00:05:23,668 --> 00:05:26,368
And six: Extensibility. Multi-source, multi-target,

97
00:05:26,218 --> 00:05:29,081
and multi-harness, so you have a choice at every major boundary.

98
00:05:30,981 --> 00:05:35,191
At launch, Rethrow will also support multi-user, multi-app workspaces.

99
00:05:35,641 --> 00:05:39,770
Bring your whole team, and manage every app, migration, and build from one place.

100
00:05:41,371 --> 00:05:44,491
Whether you're a non-technical founder, an enterprise innovation team,

101
00:05:44,341 --> 00:05:46,511
or an agency delivering client apps,

102
00:05:46,361 --> 00:05:50,572
Rethrow is your path from prototype to durable software.

103
00:05:52,272 --> 00:05:54,792
Rethrow was founded by Dustin Ellis,

104
00:05:54,642 --> 00:05:58,132
a former Amazon Web Services Senior Solutions Architect,

105
00:05:57,982 --> 00:06:00,863
and an Amazon Web Services Developer Ambassador.

106
00:06:01,313 --> 00:06:05,436
After presenting at re:Invent in December 2025, he left to build full-time.

107
00:06:05,886 --> 00:06:09,766
In 2026, the company pivoted to agent-orchestrated migrations,

108
00:06:09,616 --> 00:06:12,226
completed the Launchpad Tech Ventures summer accelerator,

109
00:06:12,076 --> 00:06:15,822
joined Launchpad Ignite, and rebranded from Vibe2Cloud to Rethrow.

110
00:06:17,622 --> 00:06:21,462
Rethrow is targeting general availability at the start of 2027,

111
00:06:21,312 --> 00:06:23,450
with early users onboarding before then.

112
00:06:23,900 --> 00:06:27,320
If you're a software reseller, startup community, or development agency,

113
00:06:27,170 --> 00:06:30,972
we'd love to talk about early access and volume-based pricing.

114
00:06:31,421 --> 00:06:35,681
Pricing, features, and agent availability are all subject to change before launch.

115
00:06:35,531 --> 00:06:39,700
Until then, our team is heads-down, building the best possible experience for you.

116
00:06:40,150 --> 00:06:44,378
Build fast, and own what comes next. Join the waitlist at rethrow.ai.
